WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.335 --> 00:00:06.737
RAY WANG: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.737 --> 00:00:08.320
We're going to spend
some time talking

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.920
about how to lead a data-driven
digital transformation.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.310
And this is what every
leader should know.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.062
I'm Ray Wang with
Constellation Research.

00:00:17.062 --> 00:00:18.520
And I want to start
with this fact.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.880
61% of the Fortune
500 have been gone.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.380
They've been merged, acquired,
gone bankrupt since 2000.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.310
Think about that.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.240
That is the pace of change that
has been happening since 2000.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.810
These are business models.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.350
These are organizations
transforming,

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.090
and in the process, taking
out the competitors.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.290
I want you to take some
time and follow along.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.070
What do you think is the hottest
performing stock since 2000?

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.540
I going to put
some names up here.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.170
You can guess, yell out loud,
tell me what you're hearing.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.500
All right, the FANGs, I'll
throw another one just for fun.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.610
Are you ready?

00:00:54.610 --> 00:01:02.290
I'm going to do the countdown,
5, 4, 3, 2, are you ready?

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.530
Here it is.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.640
Domino's Pizza, that is the
number one top-performing stock

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.060
since 2010.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:09.560
Now

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.280
This is an incredible
story of transformation.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.100
Domino's actually won the
battle for transformation.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.920
Think about it.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.430
How often do you
order a pizza a week?

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.910
And when you order that pizza,
think about it, what happens?

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.320
You're on the app.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.110
And at 4:00 PM, suddenly a
notification shows up and says,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:29.280
would you like a pizza?

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.590
And most people say yes.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.680
Now, behind the back-end
of what is happening

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.630
is a mile of data
transformation,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.550
a mile of data that's
actually happening,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.220
and a massive digital
transformation effort.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.440
Once you order that
pizza, it sets off

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.940
everything, inventory,
supply chains.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.140
It sets off the kitchen.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.432
The kitchen knows that they've
got to get this pizza in

00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:50.540
and delivered by
30 minutes or less.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.435
And you get a status.

00:01:51.435 --> 00:01:53.310
You know that the pizza
order has been taken.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.017
You know the pizza
is in the oven.

00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.600
You know the pizza
is 10 minutes away,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.058
five minutes away, and two
minutes away from your door.

00:01:59.058 --> 00:02:01.100
And in fact, you never
had the pull out any cash.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.130
You can pay
everything on the app.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.820
That is what we're
talking about.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.910
And in fact, if
you even try, you

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:10.250
can actually take a
picture of the pizza.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.680
It goes into an AMIL
engine, and they

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.320
test the quality of that pizza.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.770
That is what we're talking
about as a major transformation

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:18.690
occurs.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.880
Now, we can say Domino's won
the digital transformation war,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.402
but did it really win?

00:02:24.402 --> 00:02:26.360
And actually, there's
something that's actually

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.690
happening, because we
are seeing that this

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.420
is the foundation of every
single digital business,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.980
the ability to bring data, the
ability to bring these inputs,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.240
be able to put all this
signal intelligence in play.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.810
And the goal here, it's about
getting to precision decisions.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.170
That decision velocity becomes
a very important piece.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.680
Now why are precision
decisions so hard?

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.800
How do we get there?

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.080
How do we become successful?

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.970
Well, there's a
reason these are hard.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.800
It's really because we've
got so many data sources.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.650
There's data all over the place.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.120
It's sitting in a mainframe.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.560
It's sitting over on an app.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.250
It's in an IoT device.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.680
It's sitting on
three other clouds.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.080
And that makes it very hard.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.730
Now, that also bring
us to the point

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.520
that we have complex
data environments.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.530
These environments are crazy.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.450
And there's also
all these privacy

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.150
rules, and requirements,
and data residency.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.950
And suddenly, data is encrypted,
some data is not encrypted.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.530
We've got to pull
all this together.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.030
And then, of course, there's a
lot of external data sources,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:24.530
too.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.113
We're not just talking about the
stuff that's in your control.

00:03:27.113 --> 00:03:28.530
We're talking about
weather feeds.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.940
We're talking
about social media.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:31.280
We're talking about
videos from other places.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.810
We're talking about chats.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.370
And this has to come together.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:37.550
And then, of course, we
also have the slow process

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.250
of getting stuff into
a data warehouse,

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.200
getting into the data.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.810
And this is not easy to do,
bring all this data together.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.000
And then it gets even worse.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.760
The speed that's
required, the speed--

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.590
you want to make a
decision right away.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.480
If you're doing a
credit check right now,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.580
you're not going
to wait 10 minutes.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.650
If someone's
defrauding your ATM,

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.270
you're not going to
wait five minutes.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.520
You want to know that
something is happening.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.930
And so we've got to
bring all this together,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.400
and we've got to
do it very quickly

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.260
to get to that
precision decision.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:10.140
Now, there is a lot of change
that's going on with data.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.760
We have to understand that
this data, there's a lot of it.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.260
I'm not going to
read all the stats.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.709
You can follow along.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.769
But the most important one,
for me, is really the last one.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.500
70% of your mission
critical data

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.570
is not even in your four walls.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.790
It's coming from
external sources.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:30.570
And we've got to
[INAUDIBLE] that data.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.880
We've got to manage that data.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.005
In some cases, you're going
to transform that data,

00:04:34.005 --> 00:04:35.210
secure it, and deliver it.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.920
And it is a pain.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.690
It is not easy to do.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.120
And so we have this
insight supply chain

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.262
that starts with all the
upstream data sources.

00:04:43.262 --> 00:04:44.720
Everything comes
in, and then we've

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.130
got to bring this stuff in.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.080
We've got to figure
out what is going on.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.000
So let's classify that data.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.710
Let's transform it so it fits in
there to what we want it to do.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.920
And we've got to go augment
and add different tags,

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.190
add different sources, add
different metadata on top

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:58.790
of it.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.927
And then when we master
it, secure that data,

00:05:01.927 --> 00:05:04.010
then we've got to figure
out who to deliver it to.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.090
Where does it go?

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.190
Is it an external source?

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:06.835
Is it active?

00:05:06.835 --> 00:05:07.460
Is it inactive?

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.165
Do we take it out of the system?

00:05:09.165 --> 00:05:11.790
And then, of course, we have to
figure out how to refresh that.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.380
And that cycle goes
on and on, every time.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.660
So once we nurture
that, then we can

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.130
start thinking
about how we can get

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.590
to the data-to-decisions
lifecycle.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.840
This is extremely
important, how we

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.240
take data, all types of data,
structured, unstructured,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.440
semi-structured.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:30.810
And then when we align
that to a business process,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:31.970
It becomes powerful.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.570
Order to cash, campaign
to lead, hire to retire,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.820
incident to resolution,
procure to pay.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:39.590
And you notice
there's a line there?

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:43.640
That line shows you what
the tech team is doing.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.580
The top part is what the
business side wants to do.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.290
I want to take those
patterns, and I

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:50.590
want to figure out
what is the insight.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.370
And I want to
understand that insight.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.770
And when I understand insight,
then I want to take that,

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:57.660
and I want to take action
and make decisions.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.610
Sometimes making no
decision is a decision.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.420
But if you look at this,
it becomes a cycle.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.900
We then take that, and come
back over and over again.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.080
Now, we're doing these things
today, in many cases, in batch.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:12.380
It's taking forever, and
we've got to make decisions

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:13.810
in milliseconds.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.220
Yeah, decisions in
milliseconds, and this

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.020
is why decision
velocity is important

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:19.820
getting to precision decisions.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.830
Now one of the ways
to get there is

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.218
when we apply machine
learning and automation.

00:06:24.218 --> 00:06:25.760
And we've got to
answer that question

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.410
is, when do we automate things?

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:31.440
And we've got seven tests on
when automation makes sense

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.990
and when you apply
human-powered approaches.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.080
So when things are
highly repetitive,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.360
that's something you don't
want to have humans do.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:39.360
What do we do?

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.160
We get tired.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.220
We make mistakes.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.463
We get a little bit lazy.

00:06:42.463 --> 00:06:44.880
We get sloppy, because we're
doing it over and over again.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:45.865
We are totally bored.

00:06:45.865 --> 00:06:47.240
And that's where
you want to play

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.445
something that's AI-powered.

00:06:48.445 --> 00:06:50.570
When we think about what
actually happens with lots

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:52.140
of volume, we're overwhelmed.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.330
We can't possibly do
all that on our own.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.690
And that's another area
where we go to automation.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.240
And then, of course, when the
time to completion is quick,

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.460
well, humans don't
scale very well.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.350
When there's lots
of interaction,

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.585
humans, once again, get
overwhelmed and confused.

00:07:05.585 --> 00:07:07.070
There's so much happening.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.240
I can probably maybe
happen one, two,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.570
maybe three things
at once, and that's

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.740
really pushing the limits.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.070
But when you're interacting
with all these devices, all

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.570
these connections, and have to
make those decisions quickly,

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.090
that's where the power
of automation comes in.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.170
However, things
that are complex,

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.990
they cannot be
[INAUDIBLE] by math,

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.470
that's what we actually
see humans play a role.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:27.680
And we start to learn.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.140
We apply humans and machines,
and we bring them together.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.240
When things are
very creative that

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.580
requires to think outside
of the box, we're humans.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.640
We make the rules,
and we are really

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.115
good at breaking the rules.

00:07:39.115 --> 00:07:40.490
And once that's
in place, we also

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:41.900
think about fine motor skills.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.192
And that's another area where
automation doesn't always

00:07:44.192 --> 00:07:45.140
do a great job.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.910
But these are important
things, because we

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:48.990
have four important questions.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:50.990
These are the most
important questions

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.090
you have to answer as we
enter this autonomous decade.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.580
One, when do you trust
intelligent machine automation,

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.690
completely let go, let it
all happen with the machine?

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.550
When do we augment the
machine with a human,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.680
because we want to
understand the nuance?

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.080
We want to understand why
something isn't working, when

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.620
do you make those
exceptions, because 99.99%

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:16.258
accuracy in medicine
might not even be enough.

00:08:16.258 --> 00:08:18.800
We might actually have to get
it down to the point it's eight

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:19.520
9's.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.687
And that's going to be
interesting in an eight-sigma

00:08:21.687 --> 00:08:22.573
quality situation.

00:08:22.573 --> 00:08:23.990
And then the third
question we ask

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:26.400
is how do we augment the
human with the machine

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.310
so humans can make
quicker decisions,

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:31.530
we can make the suggestions, we
can make the recommendations?

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:34.370
And then, in what
processes do you always

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:35.562
trust human judgment?

00:08:35.562 --> 00:08:37.020
These are the
questions we're going

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.659
to play to apply to every
single business process

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:40.576
as we think about
that transformation.

00:08:40.576 --> 00:08:41.909
Now, why do you want to do this?

00:08:41.909 --> 00:08:43.760
We don't do artificial
intelligence and AI just

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:44.260
for fun.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.710
We do it because we have
outcomes we want achieve.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.200
First, tell me what's going on.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.610
What is that perception?

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.080
Then, give me a notification.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.000
Give me an alert when something
happens, because that's

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.080
way too much information.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:55.530
I can't see everything at once.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.780
So let's filter down to
the important things.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.133
And then after that, help
me make a recommendation.

00:09:00.133 --> 00:09:01.550
You've seen what
I've done before.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:02.820
Make a suggestion.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.270
And every time I reply
to that suggestion,

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:09.590
I have a digital feedback
loop, why I chose something

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:10.490
and where that goes.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:11.840
And you have the attribution.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:15.200
You've got context on when,
where, why what process

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.940
I was in, who I was with.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.440
And that helps us to learn.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.860
And over time, I want you to
automate those recommendations.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.687
And then after a while,
all that information

00:09:24.687 --> 00:09:26.270
builds on each other,
and we can start

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.130
making predictions,
and forecasts,

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.780
and understanding
what might happen.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.905
And then we get to one of
the most important things

00:09:31.905 --> 00:09:33.360
you can possibly do.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.840
It is about mitigating risk.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:37.620
Prevention is so important.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:38.810
Now, let me explain.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.650
When we look back
there used to be--

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.650
I mean, if you imagine,
you have a massive recall,

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:44.780
a massive recall.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:47.000
So one manufacturer
had a massive recall.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.290
It was like 150 phones took out
$8 billion over eight weeks.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.563
That's crazy.

00:09:53.563 --> 00:09:55.230
Now, if we could apply
machine learning,

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.770
artificial intelligence
to drive down the ability

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:01.100
to triangulate a problem, we
could take it from eight weeks

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:01.630
to one week.

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:03.380
And that would be a
billion-dollar recall.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:07.190
To go from one week to one day
would be $100 million recall.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:09.380
What's a million dollars
to solve that problem,

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:12.650
to save $100 million
and even $8 billion?

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.110
And that's where you
see the power of AI,

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.210
really, in helping
you mitigate the risk,

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:18.690
figure out compliance.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.850
One fine in GDPR could cost
you 3% of your revenues,

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.937
not profits, your revenues.

00:10:24.937 --> 00:10:27.020
And then we start to see
some of the benefits that

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:27.830
are out there.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.410
Now, what else is happening?

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.160
Well, we've got a
couple of things we have

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:32.430
to do to be able to get there.

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:36.440
We have to figure out how do we
outpace the speed of business?

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.430
This is important.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.620
The pace of change is
happening so quickly,

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.280
we need ways to catch up.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.920
We need ways to get
to that automation

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.970
for that transformation
to occur.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:47.640
The other pieces we
want to work faster,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.480
but we can't do
that in human scale.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.230
And how do we deliver
that in human scale?

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:52.560
It's going to take some time.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.920
And so we've got to figure
out when we automate

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.340
and augment our
manual processes.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:10:59.720
That's an important piece.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.680
We asked those four
questions earlier.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.650
And the reason we have
to do that is there's

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.230
some processes that will
never be automated, that have

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:07.820
to have a human intervention.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:10.640
But there's so many processes
that we have to think about

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.010
before we can bring that
automation to achieve that data

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:13.810
transformation.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.560
And then, the other
piece that's important

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.170
is the scenarios that
happen, they're complex.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.970
They're complex
business scenarios

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.000
that require some thought,
in terms of both the business

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.370
models and the monetization
models that are behind it.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.430
And then, of course, we
have to figure out how

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.090
to get to the next best action.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:31.070
How many of you
remember these books,

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:32.153
Choose Your Own Adventure?

00:11:32.153 --> 00:11:33.200
One of my favorites.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.733
Well, what is that
next best action?

00:11:34.733 --> 00:11:35.900
How do you make that choice?

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.043
How do you get there
to be successful?

00:11:38.043 --> 00:11:40.460
And then, of course, one of
the things that we can deliver

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.500
is mass personalization
at scale.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.400
That's not something
we can do manually.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.358
That's something we want
to be able to deliver,

00:11:47.358 --> 00:11:49.460
in terms of figuring out
personalization, getting

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:53.100
the preferences, getting all
the context that's required,

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.350
figuring out the restrictions.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.743
Maybe you have a food allergy.

00:11:55.743 --> 00:11:56.660
You can't eat peanuts.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:57.452
You can't eat milk.

00:11:57.452 --> 00:11:59.390
Maybe you don't have
certain types of meats

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:02.217
that you are religiously
unable to consume.

00:12:02.217 --> 00:12:04.550
And so we want to be able to
capture that and bring that

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:05.400
to life.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.733
And then, of course, there
are a other things we can do.

00:12:07.733 --> 00:12:10.010
We can do dynamic pricing
and pricing optimization,

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.270
taking networks, and
understanding demand signals,

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:13.220
supply signals.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:14.830
Using that to
figure out if we're

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.290
efficiently pricing
our offerings,

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.660
and putting that into work.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.540
And then, of course, regulatory
compliance is a big issue.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.660
Where can we actually make sure
we meet what we need to do?

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.970
If you're in
highly-regulated industries,

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:30.590
this is very
important because you

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:32.330
don't want your best
people figuring out

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:33.362
regulatory compliance.

00:12:33.362 --> 00:12:34.820
That's an area
where you want to do

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:38.210
massive amounts of automation,
massive amounts of software

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:40.310
scale, and where you
can as being preventive

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:41.960
about that on the AI side.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.330
And then we talked
about mitigating risk.

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:48.050
If we can actually mitigate
the risk of a bankruptcy, risk

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:51.260
of a bad filing, risk of
actually doing something that

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.930
takes down billions of
dollars of your company

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:56.118
or even hundreds of
dollars of your company,

00:12:56.118 --> 00:12:58.160
that's a very important
piece in figuring out how

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:00.170
to actually get that to scale.

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:01.830
So how do we get there?

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:03.350
How do we become successful?

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:06.270
Well, we've got to figure
out how to get the speed.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:08.390
Speed is so important.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.680
How do we actually
make decisions quicker?

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:13.880
There's a concept that we
call decision velocity.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.700
And I've been talking about it.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:19.640
Decision velocity is the ability
to get to the faster decisions

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.320
you can make, the
more likely you're

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:24.410
going to succeed, and even
beat, the competition.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:25.520
I'll give you an example.

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:29.540
Today, bots can actually operate
at a 100 decisions per second.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:32.390
Humans, at best, can
respond in, maybe, an hour,

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.090
but we have to go through
the organizational structure.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.400
And that might even take a
day to get to a decision.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:39.830
So if folks are making hundreds
of decisions in seconds,

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:43.400
and humans are only making a
decision, maybe, a half day,

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:44.540
we've got a problem.

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.430
And imagine, if that's
your competitors that

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:51.080
are putting automation and AI to
work, you are already, at least

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.370
exponentially, behind.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.780
So speed becomes a role.

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:55.610
Ease of use is also
super important.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.860
Our ability to actually
access that information,

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:00.620
see it, visualize it,
utilize it in a way

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.900
so that we've democratized
that information so people

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:04.980
can quickly make decisions.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.770
That visualization
is so important,

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:09.020
because that ease of use
means that I can pick it up

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.430
in any device I'm using.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:13.647
I can actually do it in any
context of a business process.

00:14:13.647 --> 00:14:15.980
And that, actually, helps me
get to that transformation.

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:17.540
And then, of course,
we have to make

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:20.390
sure these are industry
ready and business ready.

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:22.370
The name of a
customer is different

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.672
when you're in health care,
because that's a patient.

00:14:24.672 --> 00:14:26.630
When you're in legal and
professional services,

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:28.190
that's a client.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.627
And so when we think about
the role of a customer,

00:14:30.627 --> 00:14:31.710
it's very, very different.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:32.570
The language is different.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:33.778
The industries are different.

00:14:33.778 --> 00:14:35.090
The regulations are different.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.560
Now some value chains are
similar to each other.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.800
And they will collapse, and
we can work through them.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:43.670
So things, comms, media,
entertainment and telco, those

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.920
have converged because
you're selling content.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.050
You're using different
networks with distribution.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:49.790
And the technology
enables that so

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:52.790
that you can deliver
it to every customer.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:54.950
Now, contextually
relevant is important.

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.390
Now you might want to know
why I put the slide up here.

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.990
I've always wanted to use
the Easter Island slides.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:01.010
I have no idea why
these heads are here.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.885
And that's my point on
contextually relevant,

00:15:02.885 --> 00:15:03.860
like why?

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:04.940
What's the location?

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:05.780
What's the time?

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:07.040
Where am I?

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.330
What business process?

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:08.990
Am I happy?

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.040
Am I feeling great?

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.040
Did I have a great
experience before?

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:14.300
Contextually relevant
becomes an important area

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:15.223
of the attribute.

00:15:15.223 --> 00:15:16.640
And then, of course,
we want these

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:18.350
built with flexible
architecture,

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:20.090
the ability to
actually drag and drop,

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:21.902
the ability use
containers, the ability use

00:15:21.902 --> 00:15:23.360
things like
Kubernetes, the ability

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.540
to actually take microservices
and bring them together,

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:28.340
that's where it
gets very exciting.

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:30.095
And then, of course,
it has to be secure.

00:15:30.095 --> 00:15:33.680
Security is such an important
factor, especially given today

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.948
with the DDoS attacks,
the DNI attacks.

00:15:35.948 --> 00:15:37.740
All the things that
are actually happening,

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.282
we want to make sure that the
data is secure, it's encrypted,

00:15:40.282 --> 00:15:41.300
it is available to use.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:43.730
And then, of course, once
we have all that together,

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:46.350
we can start thinking
about potential use cases.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.330
So let's take a look.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:48.420
What's going on?

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.630
What are some use cases?

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:51.130
Well, the first one
is we want to be

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.390
like transform old
industries with new tech.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.940
This is about thinking about
brand-new types of business

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:56.767
models.

00:15:56.767 --> 00:15:58.350
I'm going to give
you an example here.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.740
So how many of you
actually fill out

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.410
the warranty card for a toaster?

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.830
Come on, who fills out the
warranty card for a toaster,

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:07.790
anybody?

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.510
Now, usually in a room of like
1,000, maybe 5,000 people,

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:12.260
we might get like two
or three people that

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:13.440
fill the warranty card.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.740
Now, you don't have to
answer that question.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.080
But let me explain
why it's so important.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.840
Imagine the first
phone call that's

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.447
made to the manufacturer
of that toaster.

00:16:24.447 --> 00:16:26.780
Do people ever call up and
say, oh, I love your toaster,

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:28.190
it is amazing?

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:29.660
No, they don't do that.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:33.340
That first phone call is
a call about two things.

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.110
One, my toaster is broken,
can you help me fix it,

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.940
or two, I don't know
how to use something.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:41.330
Those are typically the problems
that you get on the first call.

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.450
Now the interesting challenge
is the toaster manufacturer

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.060
does not know who the customer
is on that phone call.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:50.060
It is a horrible experience,
because for 10 minutes,

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:53.310
that poor rep is
trying to figure out,

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:54.560
where did you get the toaster?

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:55.970
What model is that toaster?

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:58.010
When did you purchase
that toaster?

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:01.270
And sometimes they figure
it's not even their toaster.

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.718
So, OK, we get this
kind of situation.

00:17:03.718 --> 00:17:05.510
But think about it,
that first phone call--

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.310
that toaster's probably $40.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.200
That first phone call
takes about 30 minutes

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:10.520
to diagnose the problem.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:13.369
You're already at
negative NPS, and you've

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:17.262
churned $5 of the profit, or
maybe even the revenue of that,

00:17:17.262 --> 00:17:19.220
and you've completely
destroyed all your profit

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:21.303
on that first phone call,
because your call center

00:17:21.303 --> 00:17:23.569
costs are, maybe, $15 an hour.

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:25.430
That is a crazy proposition.

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:30.470
So let me change the proposition
and ask you this question.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:34.010
If I told you, connect that
toaster to the internet,

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.550
and if you do that, we
will make sure we monitor

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.160
the health of that toaster,
protect your privacy,

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:42.310
all you have to do is provide
us, maybe, a text number, maybe

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.770
an email, whatever
you're comfortable with,

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:46.330
and we will monitor
that toaster.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:48.640
And if there is a defect,
we'll let you know.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:50.537
If there is a potential
problem or an upgrade

00:17:50.537 --> 00:17:52.870
that he have to tell you
about, we'll let you know, too.

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:55.730
And 90 days before that
toaster is going to die,

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:57.250
we will give you a warning.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:58.500
Would you take that offer now?

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.750
Would you register your toaster?

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:05.310
Now, if you said yes, thank
you, because now I can actually

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.280
do a bunch of things.

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:08.447
I can actually create a
whole bunch of opportunities

00:18:08.447 --> 00:18:10.813
with that toaster,
because I can say,

00:18:10.813 --> 00:18:12.480
hey, I'm remote-monitoring
your toaster,

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.700
and there's something bad
that's about to happen.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.290
Would you like to
buy a new toaster?

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:17.200
And you might take the offer.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.158
And I even give you the
offer of the same price

00:18:19.158 --> 00:18:21.150
you paid for 10 years ago.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:22.890
That's a pretty
cool offer, right?

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:24.810
And I might even do
something different.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:26.520
I might actually
say, look, would you

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.980
be interested in getting
this toaster for $0.15

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:30.372
a month for the
next eight years?

00:18:30.372 --> 00:18:31.830
We'll give you a
brand-new toaster.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.840
Well, you're thinking,
hey what is that?

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.940
Well, that's called
toast as a service.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.032
Why wouldn't you do that?

00:18:37.032 --> 00:18:39.240
And pretty soon I can get
to some interesting things.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:40.920
If you say yes on
the toaster, I might

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:42.840
make you an offer in
other kitchen appliances,

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:46.470
and say, look, I will do
your entire kitchen for $100

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:47.350
a month.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:48.970
And if any product
ever dies or fails,

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:50.220
we'll get you a brand new one.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.410
We will make sure we monitor
the health of those products.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:54.285
We'll make sure that
all the safety is there.

00:18:54.285 --> 00:18:56.650
And that's all you have
to pay, $100 a month.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:57.400
What have we done?

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.907
We've created five different
business models built on data.

00:19:00.907 --> 00:19:02.490
And this is the
digital transformation

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:03.500
we're talking about.

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:05.670
The first one, we're doing
customer experience monitoring.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.337
We're making sure
that the product's OK.

00:19:07.337 --> 00:19:08.730
We want to know what's going on.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.900
Now, I have a very
interesting question for you.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:14.010
Why do bagels cause a lot
of problems in toasters?

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:16.350
And that's really what I
really wanted to ask you.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:17.380
Why does that happen?

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:18.960
Why are you bagels
cause problems?

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:21.150
And so we can actually
see some very interesting

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:23.910
regional statistics.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.580
You will see that,
maybe, in New York,

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:30.090
the bagels are actually causing
more problems than, perhaps,

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.058
in London where you don't
see a lot of bagels.

00:19:32.058 --> 00:19:34.350
And that's the type of thing
that we're starting to do.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:36.260
And then that customer
experience monitoring,

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.760
we're trying to figure out what
are the most common problems

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.230
that are occurring.

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:46.070
Why do toasters break more in
Houston, Texas than in Dubai?

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:46.940
Think about it.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:48.398
What could it be,
what could it be?

00:19:48.398 --> 00:19:49.230
Humidity.

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.260
Something we can actually do
customer experience monitoring,

